<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1471">1471</a>–<a href="/years/1493">1493</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Topa Inca Yupanqui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Túpac <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> Yupanqui was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> from about 1471 to 1493. First as his father's general and then as ruler, he enormously enlarged the Inca Empire, conquering the Chimú kingdom on the north coast, the Cañari and peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, the Chincha and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui.jpg" alt="Túpac Inca Yupanqui" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felipe Huaman Puma de Ayala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tupac-inca-yupanqui-small.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Topa Inca Yupanqui was the tenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a>, fifth of the Hanan dynasty, and belonged to the Qhapaq Panaca, one of the clans of Inca nobles. His father was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a>, and his principal wife, or quya, was his older sister Mama Ocllo. His father made him head of the Inca army and gave him the title of Auqui, or crown prince, at a young age. In that role he launched large expeditions to the north, subduing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatun-xauxa">Hatun Xauxa</a>, the Bombon Plateau and Huaylas and besieging and capturing Curamba, Huaylla-Pucara, Canta and, most importantly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He extended the realm along the Andes through modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and developed a particular fondness for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, which he rebuilt with architects from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>. On his father's death in 1471 he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a>, and he ruled until his own death in 1493. He conquered Chimor, which held the northern coast of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and was the largest remaining rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>, and he conquered the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisuyu">Antisuyu</a> and subdued the Qulla people. In his reign he imposed rules and taxes and created two Suyuyoc Apu, governors general, one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jauja">Jauja</a> and one at Tiwanaku. The fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Saksaywaman</a>, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a> above Cusco, was built throughout his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Topa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> died about 1493 at Chincheros, leaving two legitimate sons and 90 illegitimate sons and daughters. His wife Chuqui Ocllo had persuaded him that their son Capac Huari would succeed, but he changed his mind and chose Titu Cusi Hualpa, later the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a>. Chuqui Ocllo, angered, poisoned him; she and her favourite son were killed soon after.</p><p>He is also credited with a voyage of about ten months into the Pacific around 1470, reported by Pedro Sarmiento de Gamboa, Martín de Murúa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-cabello-de-balboa">Miguel Cabello de Balboa</a>. In Sarmiento's account merchants arriving at Tumbez spoke of islands called Avachumbi and Ninachumbi, and he sailed on balsas with more than 20,000 men and returned with slaves, gold and other trophies. María Rostworowski and others identify the islands with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangareva">Mangareva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a>, though some historians doubt that the voyage took place.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Topa Inca Yupanqui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Túpac Inca Yupanqui</b>, first as general and from about 1471 as ruler, conquered the Kingdom of Chimor on the north coast and pushed the frontiers into Ecuador and south into Chile, while Huayna Capac, from about 1493, extended rule to the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chimor’s great rival proved to be the Incas, whose armies, led by <b>Topa Inca Yupanqui</b>, son of Pachacuti, overcame the kingdom around 1470.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-chimor">Kingdom of Chimor · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>There is a consensus among archaeologists that Pachacutec ordered the royal estate after his conquest of the middle and lower Urubamba, as part of a wider programme of royal estates along the river, and construction appears to date from his reign and that of <b>Túpac Inca Yupanqui</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A lineage that backed the losing side in a succession struggle could be destroyed: in 1532 Atahualpa’s generals took Cusco, burned the mummy of <b>Túpac Inca Yupanqui</b> and killed many of his lineage, who had supported Huáscar.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1463 the Inca ruler <b>Túpac Inca Yupanqui</b> conquered the Cañari after hard resistance, and the Incas built Tomebamba on the site of Guapondelig.</blockquote><a href="/state/canari-confederation">Cañari Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Inca rulers <b>Túpac Inca Yupanqui</b> and Huayna Capac fought long campaigns in the north.</blockquote><a href="/state/caranqui">Caranqui</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in Chinchero, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-13.52,-71.98&amp;year=1482" aria-label="Cusco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2577 2667 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Túpac Inca Yupanqui"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3027 2937h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-13.52,-71.98&amp;year=1482">What was here in 1482? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
